1
00:00:01,582 --> 00:00:07,748
(Q31) Over the years, attitudes towards workers have changed considerably.

2
00:00:07,748 --> 00:00:16,895
After all, there was a time when workers had no rights at all, and laboured in appalling conditions.

3
00:00:16,895 --> 00:00:24,526
Conditions have improved a lot, but conflict in the workplace is still common.

4
00:00:24,526 --> 00:00:31,967
And human resources managers nowadays need to be able to deal with it when necessary.

5
00:00:31,967 --> 00:00:35,967
What is conflict in the workplace?

6
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:54,513
[a whole range of = general category of ...+Q31答案句+much of = mostly] Definitions vary, but I'm taking it to refer to a whole range of behaviours that the victim finds unacceptable, from minor, harmless arguments to - at the opposite extreme - physical violence.&nbsp; &nbsp;Much of this is covered by the term 【bullying】,&nbsp;

7
00:00:54,513 --> 00:01:10,660
by which I mean one or more people behaving abusively or aggressively against another who is in a weaker position.

8
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:19,777
(Q32) Although all behaviour like this is a form of conflict, not all conflict can be described in these terms.

9
00:01:19,777 --> 00:01:26,011
As with all human behaviour, there are numerous reasons for it.

10
00:01:26,011 --> 00:01:41,691
[Q32答案句：A caused  by B = B is a result of  A,  show = prove] But often it's caused by someone who feels the need to show their superiority over someone else, in order to feel that they aren't at the lowest level in a hierarchy or a group of people.

11
00:01:41,691 --> 00:01:56,604
(Q33) [Q33答案句] In some cases one person simply dislikes the other, on the basis that the 【personality】 of one is in some way incompatible with that of the other person.

12
00:01:56,604 --> 00:02:11,446
(Q34) [上题Q33的解释说明，其实还没来到Q34] A general habit of optimism in one person could make them intolerant of a colleague who's constantly pessimistic - not that that justifies treating them badly, of course.

13
00:02:11,446 --> 00:02:26,709
[Q34答案句， 同义替换+疑似答案&代词指代  more interested in + more concerned about] Some conflicts arise when people are more interested in promoting themselves and their team than in the company as a whole. These conflicts are called '【structural】',&nbsp;

14
00:02:26,709 --> 00:02:41,972
and could come about, for example, when a sales team believe they are the only people in the business who do any useful work, and look down on behind-the-scenes administrators.

15
00:02:41,972 --> 00:02:57,983
(Q35) [Q35答案句， stressful=stress， result in = cause，对连接词和同替] Conflict obviously affects the individuals concerned - the situation is likely to be very stressful for victims, resulting in their absence from work, possibly for months.

16
00:02:57,983 --> 00:03:10,617
For the company, if no effort is made to deal with conflict, it can spiral out of control, and even lead to the breakdown of the business.

17
00:03:10,617 --> 00:03:22,460
(Q36) Some interesting work with chief executives - CEOs - has uncovered some of the reasons why they may treat colleagues badly.

18
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:46,539
[Q36答案句] Many CEOs combine two opposing characteristics: confidence - that is, the belief that they're capable of great achievements - with a high level of anxiety, a fear of missing targets, whether set by themselves or by the directors of the company.

19
00:03:46,539 --> 00:03:54,435
(Q37) [Q37跟定位， 隔一行] This combination can make them respond badly to anyone who questions their decisions.

20
00:03:54,435 --> 00:04:01,305
In a high pressure work environment, such characteristics become problematic.

21
00:04:01,305 --> 00:04:14,427
[Q37答案句【visions】] And it's particularly difficult to tackle the situation where colleagues, managers and board members are all trying to achieve their own visions.

22
00:04:14,427 --> 00:04:23,215
When they can't agree on strategic issues and on where they see the business going, there are real problems.

23
00:04:23,215 --> 00:04:41,516
(Q38) For managers at lower levels within the organisation, it might seem that an autocratic form of management - where the chief executive gives orders and everyone else has to obey - would see more conflict than others.

24
00:04:41,516 --> 00:04:57,984
[Q38答案句【democratic】] Interestingly, though, a company with a more democratic business model can suffer more when uncertainty about who to report to leads to conflicting demands.

25
00:04:57,984 --> 00:05:06,264
(Q39) Now I'll say a little about dealing with the type of conflict that has harmful effects.

26
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:12,328
Of course the ideal is to prevent it arising in the first place.

27
00:05:12,328 --> 00:05:24,834
[Q39答案句【respect】 a good manager = bosses, earn = gain] A good manager, at any level, will make efforts to earn the respect of the people they work with, particularly those who report to them.

28
00:05:24,834 --> 00:05:36,090
(Q40) That will involve politeness in all communications, and treating them as equals who happen to have a different role within the organisation.

29
00:05:36,090 --> 00:05:43,174
Sometimes, of course, conflict does occur, and can get out of hand.

30
00:05:43,174 --> 00:05:49,463
In such cases the human resources department often gets involved.

31
00:05:49,463 --> 00:06:04,651
[Q40答案句， external = outside the conpany] However, if one of the parties in a conflict sees human resources as simply a mouthpiece for the chief executive, then an external mediator might be able to help.

32
00:06:04,651 --> 00:06:07,151
By talking to both sides, and trying to find the truth of what's been happening, they can build a clear picture of the situation, and give feedback that both sides will accept, precisely because they're independent.

